CS631 Midterm - ls(1)
=====================

Overview
--------
This is an implementation of the ls(1) utility as described in the manual
page provided with the assignment, supporting the options
-AacdFfhiklnqRrSstuw.

Files
-----
ls.c     main(), option parsing, operand handling, directory traversal (-R)
cmp.c    comparison functions used for sorting (-S, -t, -r, -c, -u)
print.c  formatting and printing (-l, -n, -h, -k, -s, -i, -F, -q, -w)
ls.h     shared declarations (struct options, struct entry)
Makefile builds the program with -Wall -Wextra -Werror

Build and run
-------------
	$ make
	$ ./ls [-AacdFfhiklnqRrSstuw] [file ...]

Design notes
------------
* Operands are lstat(2)'ed.  A symbolic link operand that points to a
  directory is followed unless -d is given.  Non-directory operands are
  printed first, then directories; both groups are sorted separately.
* Within a directory, entries are read with readdir(3), lstat(2)'ed,
  sorted with qsort(3) (name, -S size, -t time; -r reverses), unless -f.
* When two flags conflict (-c/-u, -S/-t, -h/-k, -q/-w, -l/-n) the last one
  given wins.  -A is always set for the super-user.
* "total N" is printed for -l/-n in every directory and for -s when stdout
  is a terminal.  Block counts use 512 byte units, BLOCKSIZE, -k or -h.
* -l prints "Mon dd HH:MM" for recent files and "Mon dd  YYYY" for files
  older than six months or in the future (like the system ls).
* Non-printable characters are shown as '?' with -q (default on a
  terminal) and printed raw with -w (default otherwise).
* Memory is allocated dynamically and freed; no fixed size limits are used
  for directory contents.

How I tested
------------
I built a scratch directory containing regular files, an executable, a
hidden file, a fifo, symbolic links (to a file and to a directory), an
empty directory, nested sub-directories, a file with a non-printable
character in its name and a file with an old timestamp.  I then compared
the output of ./ls with the system ls for each option and for common
combinations (-l, -ln, -F, -i, -s, -ls, -lsi, -lS, -lt, -ltr, -lr, -R,
-lR, -lu, -lc, -a, -A, -d, -tS, -lSr), normalizing whitespace where the
system ls formats columns differently.  I also tested:
  * multiple operands (files first, then directories with "dir:" headers)
  * non-existent operands (error message on stderr, exit status 1)
  * invalid options (usage message, exit status 2)
  * unreadable directories (error message, exit status 1)
  * -h, -k, -s and the BLOCKSIZE environment variable
  * -q versus -w and conflicting flags (-qw, -wq)
  * device files (major/minor numbers in the size column)
  * running as root to confirm that -A is implied
Known, intentional differences from GNU ls: two blanks between columns,
-f does not imply -a, and ties in -t are broken by name (as the manual page
requires) rather than by sub-second timestamps.
